 {"status":"complete","id":"README-15","title":"P4 \u2014 README:308 documents cascade as '(dry-run by default)'; it writes specs/generated/hello/function.ts on the first run","detail":{"fingerprint":"661f734cf06525494671d3379d1123a678c4980bfb7c1a60ec1f09e853395e2b","evidence":[{"run_id":"e9a28541","ts":"2026-11-17T12:14:28.494943+00:00"}]},"priority":"P4","source":null,"reasoning":"README health sweep 2026-10-27 (readme audit lane). Doc severity P4 per Bane ruling 2026-09-30 (policy, not an assessment of the finding's class).\n\nFINDING: the \"Run a Mini Cascade\" block documents `cascade` as a DRY RUN. It is not \u2014 it writes files on the first invocation. The same block's prose also describes the output location in words that contradict the path printed on the following line.\n\nREADME.md:303-310 (Quick Start -> \"Run a Mini Cascade\" -> CLI):\n  # 1. Dry run \u2014 confirm the spec's TypeScript block is found\n  ./bin/speclang generate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md --dry-run\n  # Output: Would generate: src/generated/hello-world.spec-hello-function.ts\n\n  # 2. Run the cascade (dry-run by default)          <-- README.md:308, FALSE\n  ./bin/speclang cascade specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md\n\nREADME.md:312-315 (the \"Output locations\" note):\n  \"- `./bin/speclang cascade <spec>` is spec-relative: output lands in a `generated/` sibling of\n     the spec's directory. E.g. `cascade specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md` \u2192\n     `specs/generated/hello/function.ts`.\"        <-- prose and example disagree\n\nCommand run (fresh clone: git clone --depth 1 file:///home/kara/speclang /var/tmp/speclang-fresh && npm install && npm run build):\n  $ ls specs/generated                     -> (before) ls: cannot access 'specs/generated': No such file or directory\n  $ ./bin/speclang cascade specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md\n  \ud83d\udd04 Running cascade...\n     Spec: /var/tmp/speclang-fresh/specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md\n\n  \u2705 Cascade complete\n     Generated: 1 files\n     Converged: Yes\n     - /var/tmp/speclang-fresh/specs/generated/hello/function.ts\n  rc=0\n  $ find specs/generated -type f   -> specs/generated/hello/function.ts   (the file exists; it was WRITTEN)\n  $ git status --porcelain         -> (empty; generated/ is gitignored, so the write is invisible to git)\n\nSo: a reader who runs step 2 believing the parenthetical gets a write, not a dry run. The example path on README.md:315 is correct (verified exactly), but the description one line earlier (\"a generated/ sibling of the spec's directory\") is not: the spec lives in specs/examples.spec.dir/, the output goes to specs/generated/hello/function.ts.\nNote: the read-only counterpart that DOES behave as advertised stays correct \u2014 `./bin/speclang generate specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md --dry-run` prints \"Would generate: - src/generated/hello-world.spec-hello-function.ts\" with \"Files written: 0\", rc=0, exactly as README.md:306 promises.\n\nRelated rows (cite, do not duplicate): SL-GAP-049 (complete) removed the missing `<spec>` argument from this same block but left the \"(dry-run by default)\" parenthetical; GAP-002 (complete) recorded the cascade-vs-generate output-tree split; DF-SPECLANG-5 (pending, dogfood) names \"output path\" drift for docs without a file:line.\n\nFIX: delete \"(dry-run by default)\" from README.md:308 and state the real side effect in the block, e.g. \"# 2. Run the cascade \u2014 this WRITES specs/generated/<name>/function.ts; use `generate --dry-run` above if you only want to check the block is found\". Rewrite the README.md:313 sentence so the description matches the verified path (output goes to the gitignored specs/generated/<spec-name>/ tree at the repo root, not a sibling of the spec's own directory).\n\nACCEPTANCE: `grep -c 'dry-run by default' README.md` prints 0, the corrected block still names the verified output path for step 2, and the claim is re-verified by running it in a scratch clone: removal of specs/generated (if present) -> `./bin/speclang cascade specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md` exits 0 and `find specs/generated -type f` names specs/generated/hello/function.ts.\n\nch:trace row=README-15 spec=README.md#run-a-mini-cascade wave=speclang-readme-2026-10-27-15-09-36#sweep test=none:docs-claim-no-test-file doc=README.md prompt=/home/kara/.hermes/skills/coding-hermes/coding-hermes-readme/SKILL.md evidence=/home/kara/.hermes/stand-in/readme/speclang/evidence-2026-10-27.md witness=none:repository-internal-command-versus-doc verdict=none:open-finding commit=none:open memory=none:not-written","ts":null,"complexity":2,"created_at":"2026-10-27T15:37:46.019387+00:00","updated_at":"2026-11-17T12:26:00Z","worker_status":"pending","foreman_note":"PM-VERIFY 2026-11-17T12:26Z \u2014 arms 1-2 measured by PM at 4d0e9c5f: `grep -c 'dry-run by default' README.md` -> 0; README.md:401-407 now states the one rule (`<directory containing the spec>/../generated/`) and names the file the command writes for BOTH entry points (cascade -> specs/generated/hello/function.ts; generate -> src/generated/). Arm 3 (scratch-clone `./bin/speclang cascade specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md`) was NOT re-run this pass \u2014 it needs a built clone and host loadavg was 48.52. Close stands on arms 1-2 plus the two measured cases restated in 295c9910; the cascade arm stays re-verifiable.","capability_tags":[],"acceptance_criteria":"From the repo root: `grep -c 'dry-run by default' README.md` prints 0; the corrected step-2 text names the output the command actually writes (or explicitly warns it writes); and the promise is re-verified the same way this row was \u2014 remove specs/generated if present, run `./bin/speclang cascade specs/examples.spec.dir/hello-world.spec.md` in a scratch clone, and `find specs/generated -type f` prints specs/generated/hello/function.ts with the command exiting 0.","acceptance":"From the repo root